{% render 'section-style', section: section %}
{%- comment -%}
  Product page, rebuilt against the design (design-reference/product.html).

  Structure, in order:
    1. 50/50 gallery + buy box            (the design's `twoCols`, not a sidebar)
    2. spec strip — 4 cells, ruled top and bottom, divided
    3. process — numbered steps + ingredients | full-bleed process image
    4. pour it with — 3 bordered pairing cards
    5. goes well in a case with — horizontal related cards

  `render` is a standalone tag and cannot appear inside {% liquid %}, so every
  capture that wraps one sits outside the liquid block.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- capture code %}{% render 'non-code', product: product %}{% endcapture -%}
{%- capture calories %}{% render 'non-nutrition', product: product, label: 'Calories per serve' %}{% endcapture -%}
{%- capture sugars %}{% render 'non-nutrition', product: product, label: 'Sugars - Total' %}{% endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  assign m = product.metafields.custom
  assign code = code | strip
  assign calories = calories | strip
  assign sugars = sugars | strip

  # Resolved once here rather than only in the buy box, because EVERY bottle
  # assumption below has to answer to it. The Cap shipped reading
  # "NON DAD CAP - 0.0% ABV - 750ML", with a somm offering to discuss its
  # pairing and sugar, TASTES showing "NON Cap" and SERVE telling a customer to
  # chill a hat in a wine glass. None of that is a design gap; it is a page
  # stating things that are not true, because one template renders a 750ml
  # sparkling and a piece of headwear.
  assign not_drink = m.not_a_drink | metafield_text

  assign tastes = m.profile | metafield_text | default: m.tastes | metafield_text
  assign sits = m.sits | metafield_text

  # IS THIS ONE BOTTLE?
  #
  # not_a_drink already excused the Cap and the Stopper. It does not excuse the
  # sets, and the sets are drinks — so every bottle-shaped assumption on this
  # page fired on them and was wrong in a different way each time:
  #
  #   The Everyday Set is three 750ml bottles. Its eyebrow read
  #   "SET - 0.0% ABV - 750ml", which is the volume of a third of it.
  #   Its spec strip rendered exactly one cell, "Serve: Chilled, wine glass",
  #   because that cell is a hardcoded default rather than data, so a strip
  #   designed for four cells shipped holding one.
  #   And its Somm panel opened "I've read the whole spec sheet for THIS
  #   BOTTLE", then, asked about sugar, replied "which bottle did you have in
  #   mind?" — a dead end on a page that names its three bottles six lines up.
  #
  # The distinguishing fact is not the product type or the title; it is whether
  # this product carries a single bottle's spec data. Bottles have profile,
  # sits, ingredients and nutrition. Sets, accessories and the gift card have
  # none of them. So that is the test, and it is read from the data rather than
  # guessed from a name — nothing here breaks when a set is called The Spice
  # Set instead of Spice Pack.
  assign has_spec = false
  if m.profile != blank or m.tastes != blank or m.sits != blank or m.ingredients != blank
    assign has_spec = true
  endif

  # 'Chilled, wine glass' is a hardcoded house default for a field that is null
  # on every product, so it was never this bottle's instruction - and on an
  # accessory it is simply wrong. Accessories get nothing rather than a guess.
  # Sets too: a one-cell spec strip reads as a broken four-cell one.
  assign serve = m.serve | metafield_text
  if serve == blank and not_drink == blank and has_spec
    assign serve = 'Chilled, wine glass'
  endif
  assign ingredients = m.ingredients | metafield_text
  assign style = m.style | metafield_text

  assign nutrition = ''
  if calories != blank and sugars != blank
    assign nutrition = calories | append: ' calories, ' | append: sugars | append: ' sugar'
  endif

  assign spec_count = 2
  if nutrition != blank
    assign spec_count = 3
  endif
  if sits != blank
    assign spec_count = spec_count | plus: 1
  endif
-%}

<div class="non-product" data-non-somm-root>
  {%- comment -%} ── gallery ───────────────────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="non-gallery">
    {%- if product.media.size > 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A LOW-RESOLUTION VERSION OF THE ACTUAL SHOT, behind the real one.

        The space was already reserved, so nothing jumped — but what filled it
        was a flat cream rectangle, and on a phone that rectangle sat there long
        enough to read as a broken image rather than a loading one. A blank
        placeholder and a failure look identical; a blurred bottle does not.

        32px wide is roughly 400 bytes and arrives with the HTML's own
        connection, so it paints effectively immediately. The full image loads
        on top of it and covers it — no fade, no JavaScript, nothing to go
        wrong. The cream stays underneath as the base colour for the moment
        before even that lands.

        Passed as a style attribute rather than a class because the URL is
        per-product, and a CSS variable is the only way to hand Liquid data to
        a stylesheet without writing a rule per bottle.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture ph_style -%}
        --non-ph: url({{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 32, format: 'webp' }});
      {%- endcapture -%}
      {{
        product.featured_media
        | image_url: width: 1400, format: 'webp'
        | image_tag:
          loading: 'eager',
          fetchpriority: 'high',
          widths: '400,600,900,1200,1400',
          sizes: '(min-width: 860px) 46vw, 100vw',
          class: 'non-gallery__main',
          data-non-gallery-main: '',
          style: ph_style,
          alt: product.featured_media.alt
          | default: product.title
      }}

      {%- if product.media.size > 1 -%}
        <div class="non-gallery__thumbs">
          {%- for media in product.media limit: 8 -%}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="non-gallery__thumb"
              data-non-gallery-thumb
              data-full="{{ media | image_url: width: 1400, format: 'webp' }}"
              aria-current="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
              aria-label="View image {{ forloop.index }}">
              <img src="{{ media | image_url: width: 200, format: 'webp' }}" alt="" loading="lazy">
            </button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'non-gallery__main' }}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Ingredients, under the pictures. It used to close the process band's copy
      column, where it made that column taller and cost the film beside it
      picture — the media is cropped to the copy's height. Here it fills the
      space under the gallery that the buy column was already leaving empty,
      and it reads where a customer looks for it: with the bottle, not with
      the method. Set larger than it was, because it is now a section of the
      page rather than a footnote to one.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign ingredients_list = m.ingredients | metafield_text -%}
    {%- if ingredients_list != blank -%}
      <div class="non-ingr">
        <div class="non-spec__k">{{ section.settings.ingredients_label }}</div>
        <p class="non-ingr__list">{{ ingredients_list }}</p>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} ── buy box ───────────────────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="non-buy">
    <div>
      <div class="non-mono non-eyebrow">
        {{ code }}
        {%- if style != blank %} &middot; {{ style }}{% endif -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The suffix is a SECTION setting — one string for every product — so
          "0.0% ABV · 750ml" was printed on the Stopper and the Waiter's Friend
          too. A metal bottle stopper does not have an ABV or a volume, and
          stating one is a factual error on a product page, not a cosmetic one.

          Per-product override first (custom.spec_line), then the section
          default, and nothing at all when the product carries
          custom.not_a_drink. An accessory gets its code and its style and stops
          there.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign not_drink = product.metafields.custom.not_a_drink | metafield_text -%}
        {%- assign spec_line = product.metafields.custom.spec_line | metafield_text -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          has_spec, not just not_drink. The suffix is one bottle's ABV and
          volume, so it belongs only to products that ARE one bottle. A set of
          three 750ml bottles printing "750ml" is the same class of error as
          the Stopper printing an ABV, and it was live on five set pages.
          A per-product custom.spec_line still overrides everything, which is
          where "3 x 750ml" should go once someone writes it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if spec_line != blank -%}
          &middot; {{ spec_line }}
        {%- elsif not_drink == blank and has_spec and section.settings.eyebrow_suffix != blank -%}
          &middot; {{ section.settings.eyebrow_suffix }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      <h1 class="non-buy__name">{{ product.title | remove: code | strip }}</h1>

      {%- if m.blurb != blank -%}
        <p class="non-buy__blurb">{{ m.blurb | metafield_text }}</p>
      {%- elsif m.profile != blank -%}
        <p class="non-buy__blurb">{{ m.profile | metafield_text }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        THE SHOPIFY DESCRIPTION, FOR PRODUCTS THE DESIGN LEAVES EMPTY.

        Every product carries two to four kilobytes of written description in
        Shopify and this template renders none of it. On a bottle that is the
        right call and was a deliberate one: the design replaced a prose block
        with the spec strip, the five process steps and three pairing cards,
        all of which say the same things better and none of which duplicate it.

        On a set it is a hole. A set has no profile, no process and no pairings
        — has_spec is false, so all of those bands correctly sit out — and what
        remains is a title, a price and a photograph. Meanwhile the description
        holds the only fact unique to the product: which three bottles are in
        the box. Five sets shipped without it while it sat in Shopify the whole
        time. Nothing needed writing; it needed printing.

        Gated on has_spec so bottles are untouched. Gated on blurb being empty
        so a product with a proper short blurb is not given both.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Rendered as the same card grid the bottles use for custom.benefits,
        not as a wall of prose. Printing the description raw was the right
        first move — the copy existed and the page was not showing it — but
        what it showed was CONTAINS / ABOUT / PROFILE with bullets underneath,
        which reads as a different website sitting next to the Stopper's page.
        desc-cards turns each heading into a card. See that snippet for how,
        and for its one assumption about heading markup.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if has_spec == false and m.blurb == blank and product.description != blank -%}
        {% render 'desc-cards', html: product.description, label: section.settings.benefits_label %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <form method="post" action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" data-non-product-form>
      <input type="hidden" name="form_type" value="product">
      <input type="hidden" name="utf8" value="✓">
      <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}" data-non-variant-input>

      {%- if product.variants.size > 1 -%}
        <div class="non-variants" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Pack size">
          {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign units = variant.metafields.custom.units | metafield_text | plus: 0
              if units == 0
                assign units = variant.title | plus: 0
              endif
              if units == 0
                assign units = 1
              endif
              assign each = variant.price | divided_by: units
            -%}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="non-variant"
              role="radio"
              data-non-variant
              data-variant-id="{{ variant.id }}"
              data-price="{{ variant.price }}"
              aria-checked="{% if variant.id == product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
              {% unless variant.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
              <span class="non-variant__lines">
                <span class="non-variant__label">
                  {%- comment -%}
                    Pack names come from Pack label blocks, matched on the
                    variant title, so "6" can read "6 pack" without anyone
                    renaming variants in the product admin — variant titles are
                    shared with reporting and checkout and should stay plain.
                    Falls back to the variant's own title when no block matches.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- liquid
                    assign pack_label = ''
                    for b in section.blocks
                      if b.type == 'pack' and b.settings.variant_title == variant.title
                        assign pack_label = b.settings.label
                        break
                      endif
                    endfor
                    if pack_label == blank and variant.title == 'Default Title'
                      assign pack_label = product.title
                    endif
                    if pack_label == blank
                      assign pack_label = variant.title
                    endif
                    echo pack_label
                  -%}
                </span>
                <span class="non-variant__per">
                  {%- if variant.available -%}
                    {{ each | money }} {{ section.settings.each_word }}
                    {%- if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price -%}
                      {%- assign save = variant.compare_at_price | minus: variant.price | times: 100 | divided_by: variant.compare_at_price -%}
                      &middot; {{ section.settings.save_word }} {{ save }}%
                    {%- endif -%}
                  {%- else -%}
                    {{ 'products.product.sold_out' | t }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                </span>
              </span>
              <span class="non-variant__price">{{ variant.price | money }}</span>
            </button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">

      <button type="submit" class="non-atc" {% unless product.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
        {%- if product.available -%}
          {{ section.settings.add_label }} &mdash; <span data-non-price>{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.price | money }}</span>
        {%- else -%}
          {{ 'products.product.sold_out' | t }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </button>

      {%- comment -%}
        Back in stock. A disabled button is a dead end — the Cap and the Beanie
        are both at zero with a DENY policy, so every visitor who wants one
        currently leaves with nothing to show for it.

        Klaviyo's CLIENT endpoint, keyed on the public site ID. That key is
        public by design (it is the same one the onsite script exposes), so it
        belongs in a theme setting rather than in a Worker — there is no secret
        here to protect and no server round trip to add.

        Rendered only when the variant cannot be bought, and the whole block is
        omitted if no key is set, so an unconfigured store shows the plain
        sold-out button it shows today rather than a form that silently fails.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if product.available == false and section.settings.klaviyo_public_key != blank -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A DIV, not a form, and that is not a style choice.

          This sits inside the product's own <form>. HTML forbids nested forms,
          so the parser DROPS the inner start tag and keeps its children — the
          markup is present in the source and `[data-non-bis]` does not exist
          in the DOM, so the script binds to nothing and the field is inert
          while looking perfectly correct. Verified on the Cap PDP: the class
          string was in outerHTML and querySelector returned null.

          Submission is a button click plus Enter on the input, handled in
          back-in-stock.js.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="non-bis"
          data-non-bis
          data-bis-key="{{ section.settings.klaviyo_public_key | escape }}"
          data-bis-variant="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}">
          <div class="non-bis__label non-mono">{{ section.settings.bis_heading }}</div>
          <div class="non-bis__row">
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="bis-{{ section.id }}">{{ section.settings.bis_placeholder }}</label>
            <input
              id="bis-{{ section.id }}"
              class="non-bis__input"
              type="email"
              name="email"
              required
              autocomplete="email"
              placeholder="{{ section.settings.bis_placeholder | escape }}"
              data-non-bis-input>
            <button type="button" class="non-bis__submit" data-non-bis-submit>{{ section.settings.bis_cta }}</button>
          </div>
          <p class="non-bis__msg" data-non-bis-msg role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
        </div>
        <script src="{{ 'back-in-stock.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%} The reassurance row from the design, under the button. {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="non-buy__trust">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- if block.type == 'trust' -%}
            <span {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>{{ block.settings.text }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

      <p class="non-error" data-non-form-error></p>
    </form>

    {%- comment -%}
      FEATURES & BENEFITS, per product.

      Reads custom.benefits — one per line, "Title | Body" — so every product
      carries its own and nothing is hardcoded to the bottles. The Stopper's
      case for existing is nothing like NON1's, and a shared section setting
      would print one of them on the other, which is exactly the mistake the ABV
      eyebrow was making one element up.

      Falls back to the section's blocks when a product has no metafield, so a
      merchandiser can fill this in from the theme editor before the data
      exists. Renders nothing when neither is present — an empty band is worse
      than no band.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign benefits_raw = product.metafields.custom.benefits | metafield_text -%}
    {%- assign benefit_blocks = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'benefit' -%}
    {%- if benefits_raw != blank or benefit_blocks.size > 0 -%}
      <div class="non-benefits">
        {%- if section.settings.benefits_label != blank -%}
          <div class="non-mono non-eyebrow">{{ section.settings.benefits_label }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}
        <div class="non-benefits__list">
          {%- if benefits_raw != blank -%}
            {%- assign rows = benefits_raw | newline_to_br | split: '<br />' -%}
            {%- for row in rows -%}
              {%- assign clean = row | strip -%}
              {%- if clean != blank -%}
                {%- assign bits = clean | split: '|' -%}
                <div class="non-benefit">
                  <div class="non-benefit__t">{{ bits[0] | strip }}</div>
                  {%- if bits.size > 1 -%}
                    <p class="non-benefit__b">{{ bits[1] | strip }}</p>
                  {%- endif -%}
                </div>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- else -%}
            {%- for block in benefit_blocks -%}
              <div class="non-benefit" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
                <div class="non-benefit__t">{{ block.settings.title }}</div>
                {%- if block.settings.body != blank -%}
                  <p class="non-benefit__b">{{ block.settings.body }}</p>
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} ── somm, as a panel ────────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The somm is briefed on a bottle - its opener promises "I've read the
      whole spec sheet for this bottle" and its seeded questions are about
      sweetness and de-alcoholisation. Pointed at a stopper or a cap it will
      still answer confidently, and be wrong. Off for anything not a drink
      until it has a brief for one.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      has_spec for the same reason the opener gives: the panel says it has read
      the spec sheet for THIS BOTTLE, and on a set there is no such sheet. Asked
      a spec question with code SET the Worker correctly refuses to guess and
      replies "which bottle did you have in mind?" — safe, and a dead end. Off
      until a set has a brief of its own, exactly as accessories are.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if section.settings.show_somm and not_drink == blank and has_spec -%}
      <div class="non-sommbox" style="{% if section.settings.somm_dot_colour != blank %}--non-somm-dot: {{ section.settings.somm_dot_colour }};{% endif %}{% if section.settings.somm_bg != blank %}--non-somm-bg: {{ section.settings.somm_bg }};{% endif %}{% if section.settings.somm_border != blank %}--non-somm-border: {{ section.settings.somm_border }};{% endif %}">
        {%- comment -%}
          One label, not two. The head printed the locale string "NON Somm" and
          the opening line printed section.settings.somm_title immediately below
          it — the same words twice, six lines apart. The setting now drives the
          single remaining label, so the panel is named in one place and that
          place is editable.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="non-sommbox__head">
          {%- comment -%}
            NOT `setting | default: 'key' | t`. Liquid applies `t` to whatever
            the chain produces, so a set value gets looked up as a translation
            key and renders "Translation missing: en.NON Somm". The locale
            string is only a fallback for when the setting is empty.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="non-mono non-eyebrow">
            {%- if section.settings.somm_title != blank -%}
              {{ section.settings.somm_title }}
            {%- else -%}
              {{ 'products.product.somm_heading' | t }}
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
          <span class="non-sommbox__live">
            <span class="non-sommbox__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {{ section.settings.somm_status }}
          </span>
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          The opener was wrapped in .non-chat__msg--a, a chat bubble with its own
          border and background sitting inside the panel's border — a box in a
          box, with the bubble's min-height leaving a large empty area beneath
          three lines of text. It is not a message; nobody sent it. It reads as
          the panel's own standfirst, so it is one now.

          The transcript wrapper stays: somm.js appends real replies into it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="non-sommbox__body" data-non-somm-transcript>
          <p class="non-sommbox__opener">{{ section.settings.somm_opener }}</p>
          <p class="non-somm__stream" data-non-somm-stream hidden></p>
        </div>

        <form
          class="non-sommbox__ask"
          data-non-somm
          data-somm-context="product"
          data-somm-code="{{ code }}">
          <label class="visually-hidden" for="somm-p-{{ section.id }}">{{ section.settings.somm_placeholder }}</label>
          <input
            id="somm-p-{{ section.id }}"
            class="non-somm__input"
            type="search"
            autocomplete="off"
            data-non-somm-input
            placeholder="{{ section.settings.somm_placeholder | escape }}">
          <button type="submit" class="non-sommbox__submit">{{ 'products.product.ask' | t }}</button>
        </form>

        {%- assign seeds = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'seed' -%}
        {%- if seeds.size > 0 -%}
          <div class="non-sommbox__seeds">
            {%- for block in seeds -%}
              <button
                type="button"
                class="non-somm__seed"
                data-non-somm-seed
                data-answer="{{ block.settings.answer | escape }}"
                {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
                {{ block.settings.label }}
              </button>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="non-somm__answer" data-non-somm-answer hidden>
          <div class="non-somm__picks" data-non-somm-picks hidden></div>
          {% render 'ask-form' %}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{%- comment -%} ── spec strip ────────────────────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Block-driven. This was four fixed settings — spec_tastes, spec_sits,
  spec_nutrition, spec_serve — which meant the labels were editable but the
  strip itself was not: you could not add a fifth row, drop one you did not
  want, or change their order without editing Liquid.

  Each block carries a label and a SOURCE, because the values come from product
  metafields rather than from the theme. `custom` is there so a row can be
  written by hand for a product that has no metafield for it.

  A row whose source resolves to nothing is skipped rather than rendered empty,
  which is what the old `if sits != blank` guards were doing by hand. The count
  is derived from what actually rendered, so the layout modifier stays honest.

  If no spec blocks exist the four original settings still render, so a template
  that has not been migrated looks exactly as it did before.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign specs = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'spec' -%}

{%- if specs.size > 0 -%}
  {%- capture spec_cells -%}
    {%- for block in specs -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign val = ''
        case block.settings.source
          when 'tastes'
            # NOT `default: product.title`. An empty tastes field fell back to
            # the product's own name, so the Cap's TASTES cell read "NON Cap" -
            # a label presented as a flavour note. custom.profile and
            # custom.tastes are null on every product today, so this fallback
            # was firing on the whole catalogue, not just accessories. Blank
            # means the cell does not render.
            assign val = tastes
          when 'sits'
            assign val = sits
          when 'nutrition'
            assign val = nutrition
          when 'serve'
            assign val = serve
          when 'custom'
            assign val = block.settings.custom_value
          else
            assign val = block.settings.custom_value
        endcase
      -%}
      {%- if val != blank -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The icon is derived from the block's SOURCE, not chosen per block.
          The source already says what the cell is — tastes, sits, nutrition,
          serve — so a separate picker would be a second place to keep the same
          fact, and the two would drift. A custom cell gets no icon rather than
          a wrong one.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- liquid
          case block.settings.source
            when 'tastes'
              assign cell_icon = 'palate'
            when 'sits'
              assign cell_icon = 'seat'
            when 'nutrition'
              assign cell_icon = 'nutrition'
            when 'serve'
              assign cell_icon = 'serve'
            else
              assign cell_icon = ''
          endcase
        -%}
        <div class="non-specstrip__cell" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
          <div class="non-spec__k non-spec__k--icon">
            {%- if cell_icon != blank -%}{% render 'stat-icon', icon: cell_icon %}{%- endif -%}
            <span>{{ block.settings.label }}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="non-specstrip__v">{{ val }}</div>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- assign rendered_count = spec_cells | split: 'non-specstrip__cell' | size | minus: 1 -%}
  {%- if rendered_count > 0 -%}
    <div class="non-specstrip non-specstrip--{{ rendered_count }}">{{ spec_cells }}</div>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- else -%}
  <div class="non-specstrip non-specstrip--{{ spec_count }}">
    <div class="non-specstrip__cell">
      <div class="non-spec__k">{{ section.settings.spec_tastes }}</div>
      <div class="non-specstrip__v">{{ tastes }}</div>
    </div>

    {%- if sits != blank -%}
      <div class="non-specstrip__cell">
        <div class="non-spec__k">{{ section.settings.spec_sits }}</div>
        <div class="non-specstrip__v">{{ sits }}</div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if nutrition != blank -%}
      <div class="non-specstrip__cell">
        <div class="non-spec__k">{{ section.settings.spec_nutrition }}</div>
        <div class="non-specstrip__v">{{ nutrition }}</div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="non-specstrip__cell">
      <div class="non-spec__k">{{ section.settings.spec_serve }}</div>
      <div class="non-specstrip__v">{{ serve }}</div>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The bottle's own spec sheet, for the Somm.

  The panel's opening line promises "I've read the whole spec sheet for this
  bottle" and until now the request carried only a product CODE — so a question
  like "how long will it last after I open it?" had nothing behind it to answer
  from. The fact was already in Shopify on every bottle (custom.storage); it was
  simply never sent.

  Only fields that already exist and are already shown to a customer somewhere
  on this page, so this adds no new claim — it hands the Somm the same sheet the
  page is printing.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script type="application/json" data-non-somm-facts>
  {
    "code": {{ code | json }},
    "title": {{ product.title | json }},
    "price": {{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.price | money | json }},
    "profile": {{ tastes | default: '' | json }},
    "sits": {{ sits | default: '' | json }},
    {%- comment -%}
      The RAW metafield, not `serve`. `serve` carries a hardcoded
      'Chilled, wine glass' fallback for the spec strip, and custom.serve is
      currently null on every product — so sending `serve` here would hand the
      Somm a generic house default as though it were this bottle's own serving
      instruction. Empty means absent, and the Worker's facts prompt already
      says to admit when the sheet does not cover something.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    "serve": {{ m.serve | metafield_text | default: '' | json }},
    "ingredients": {{ ingredients | default: '' | json }},
    "storage": {{ m.storage | metafield_text | default: '' | json }},
    "notes": {{ m.notes | metafield_text | default: '' | json }},
    "nutrition": {{ nutrition | default: '' | json }},
    {%- comment -%}
      PACK PRICING, so arithmetic stops being a deflection.

      The accuracy test asked "do I save anything buying six" thirty times and
      got the price redirect thirty times — while the answer is knowable, and
      on these bottles it is "no": six at $150 is 6 x $30 exactly. Deflecting a
      question whose answer is sitting in the payload reads as evasion, and the
      customer is on the page where the number is printed anyway.

      Sent as the variant list rather than as a computed saving, so the Worker
      does the sum from real figures and nothing here can go stale against the
      variant it describes.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    "packs": [
      {%- for v in product.variants -%}
        {"units": {{ v.title | plus: 0 | default: 1 | json }}, "price": {{ v.price | money | json }}, "available": {{ v.available | json }}}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ],
    "available": {{ product.available | json }}
  }
</script>

<script type="application/json" data-non-catalogue>
  {
    {%- assign seen = '' -%}
    {%- for p in collections['the-range'].products -%}
      {%- capture c %}{% render 'non-code', product: p %}{% endcapture -%}
      {%- assign c = c | strip -%}
      {%- assign marker = '|' | append: c | append: '|' -%}
      {%- unless seen contains marker -%}
        {%- assign seen = seen | append: marker -%}
        {%- unless forloop.first %},{% endunless -%}
        {{ c | json }}: {
          "title": {{ p.title | json }},
          "url": {{ p.url | json }},
          "image": {{ p.featured_image | image_url: width: 200, format: 'webp' | json }},
          "price": {{ p.price | money | json }},
          "note": {{ p.metafields.custom.profile | metafield_text | default: '' | truncate: 44 | json }},
          "variantId": {{ p.selected_or_first_available_variant.id | json }},
          "available": {{ p.available | json }}
        }
      {%- endunless -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  }
</script>

<script src="{{ 'somm.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ 'product.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

